WEBVTT

p0.1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.533
This video shows that in doeff, a library for algebraic effects in Python,

p0.2
00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:08.967
a handler decides how a paused function goes on.

p0.3
00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:15.400
A doeff handler receives k, the rest of that function. We compare it with an asyncio task.

p1.1
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:22.867
When an asyncio task pauses, the loop has two moves: send the result once the future is done,

p1.2
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.250
or throw CancelledError into it.

p2
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:34.500
A doeff handler receives k, the rest of the paused function. What happens next is the handler's decision.

p3
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.100
Resume k with a value, and the function goes on from its yield.

p4
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:48.300
Throw into k, and the function sees the exception at its yield, where its own except clause can catch it.

p5.1
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.550
Keep k for later, and you have concurrency.

p5.2
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:58.050
scheduled holds the k of each task and goes back to it when the task can go on.

p6.1
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.600
Or never resume k.

p6.2
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:08.150
The run ends with the handler's own value, and the paused generators are closed, so their finally blocks run.

p7
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:14.850
Neither model goes on twice. k is one-shot, like a coroutine object.

p8.1
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.567
But greet() gives a program, not a paused run.

p8.2
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:25.250
Each run starts a new generator, so one program runs under ada, then under grace.
